HVGpCqVa3-y6_KuJE_wGHv2M5YtSUTbQJAfae8utabTaqenNkHq9tCjzYKuJFJq2kXIH9eFeV0e=
Q_lEqOSu7MOWdN4p52pDH7p3Rpqmmf5zT07s4-BRmWKotOF1EFTXijKaoK4uN9Y6QEqe3wSTRKD=
wBvx6kkQSB8T9reF0XlpD-wwfcPybSrE9j1ZQHxrEu" class=3D"css-sdwaa1" style=3D"c=
olor:#286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-decoration:underline">c=
riticized the European Union</a><span> for leading =E2=80=9Can energy war a=
gainst Russia, when we haven=E2=80=99t got any energy.=E2=80=9D</span></p><=
p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px">=
<span>Putin has long believed that his commitment to waging war on Ukraine =
would outlast the commitment of Western allies to defending Ukraine.</span>=
</p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 1=
5px"><span>It=E2=80=99s cost him more than a million troops killed or wound=
ed, and counting. But his strategy might finally be paying off.</span></p><=
p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px">=
<span style=3D"font-weight:700">Putin=E2=80=99s waiting game</span></p><p s=
tyle=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><sp=
an>When Putin launched the full invasion of Ukraine in 2022, he expected it=
 to be a quick war. One reason that didn=E2=80=99t happen was the resolve a=
nd unity of Ukrainians to put up a fight. Another was the resolve and unity=
 of Western allies to help.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geo=
rgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I spoke with my colleague Anto=
n Troianovski, who covered the invasion as Moscow bureau chief and is now o=
ur global affairs correspondent. Anton told me that Putin was taken by surp=
rise that Western countries managed to construct a united front.</span></p>=
<p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"=
><span>That meant not only supplying Ukraine with weapons, money and intell=
igence. It also meant weaning European countries off cheap Russian gas at a=
 major economic cost to Moscow, but to Europe as well. Germany, the contine=
nt=E2=80=99s biggest economy, relied on Russia for more than half the gas i=
t used before the invasion; it now pays more for gas from other countries.<=
/span></p><table border=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" style=3D"=
width:100%"><tbody><tr><td style=3D"text-align:center"><img width=3D"500" h=
eight=3D"624" style=3D"display:inline-block;width:83%;max-width:500px;heigh=
